  "query_preview": "..\nIssued 1154 PM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nFriday through Tuesday:\n\nThat upper trough will slowly track across eastern MN, WI, \nnorthern IL, and MI/IN from Friday through Saturday. There is\nonly weak support of this trough in the jet stream level. At the\nsurface, low pressure will track from the SD/NE border Friday\nmorning, through IA on Friday, and across IL Fri nt into Sat.\nThe very slow movement of this system is due to the weak upper\ndynamics. The surface low also gets quite displaced from the mid\nlevel low during this time. Model profiles show the potential\nfor weak and shallow instability at times, but nothing near\nstrong/unstable. The chance for lightning is questionable for\nsouthern WI given these profiles. \n\nWhile all of this suggests a very weak system for the Upper \nMidwest, the models are all producing high amounts of precip. I\nam hesitant to buy into this idea given the lack of convection\nor strong synoptic forcing. The leading edge of the precip is\nexpected to move into south central WI by mid Friday morning and\nreach the lakeshore of southeast WI around mid afternoon. It \nwill be encountering dry air, but we are expecting a solid \nperiod of a saturated column of air to affect all areas of\nsouthern WI between Fri afternoon and Saturday morning. \n\nSo confidence is increasing for a period of steady LIGHT rain, \nbut I am not expecting a drought-busting rain. Several ensembles\nhave only a 35% chance of exceeding a half inch in 24 hr north \nof I-94, with higher confidence (up to 85%) south of there. This\nmight be too generous based on the anticipated rainfall rates. \nThere should be a pretty tight gradient of precip totals across \nsouthern WI by the end of this event, ranging from highest near \nthe WI/IL border and lowest toward east central WI. \n\nThe upper trough and resultant rain should clear south central\nWI by midday Saturday and southeast WI by at least late\nafternoon. High pressure will spread back into southern WI on\nSunday and dominate through Tuesday. A weak upper trough will \ntry to bring some light rain or sprinkles to the state on \nTuesday afternoon/night, but that is looking weak and dry. \n\nCronce",
